The same list as \code{el_events("el_table")}, which says how an event's arguments travel.

Rendered into \code{el_table_output("tbl")}, the inputs are \code{input$tbl_...}.
\code{input$tbl} itself is not used: Element's table has no value of its own
(no \code{v-model}), and the name stays free.

An edit is shown at once and applied to the server's copy of the data,
so \code{\link[=el_table_data]{el_table_data()}} and \code{input$tbl_selection_change} see it; an
observer of \code{input$tbl_cell_edit} saves it, or refuses it by putting the
old value back: \code{update_el_table(session, "tbl", replace = row, at = input$tbl_cell_edit$row)}.

Row numbers count rows of the data the table shows, whatever the user's
sort -- the data \code{\link[=el_table_data]{el_table_data()}} returns. Rendering the same data again
keeps the user's ticks, sort and open rows; new data clears the
selection, as Element does, unless rows carry a key: \code{row_key} and a
selection column with \code{reserve_selection = TRUE} keep the ticked rows
that are still there. Rows inserted, replaced or deleted with
\code{\link[=update_el_table]{update_el_table()}} leave the other rows' ticks alone.

With tree data, \code{lazy = TRUE} and no \code{load} of your own, the server loads
a row's children when it is opened: \verb{input$<id>_load} asks, with \code{key}
(the row's \code{row_key} field), \code{row} and \code{request}; answer with
\code{\link[=el_load_children]{el_load_children()}}. A row with children to load carries
\code{hasChildren = TRUE}.
}

\section{Row actions}{

A button in a \code{cell} reports back with \code{rowAction()}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{list(label = "", cell = el$button(size = "small",
  "@click" = "rowAction('edit', scope)", "Edit"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

sets \verb{input$<id>_edit} to \code{list(row_index =, row =)} -- the 1-based row
number, to index your own data with, and the row as the table holds it. It
is an event input: clicking the same row twice reports twice.
}

